---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Q7 = `Four independent tasks:

  Task One      20 seconds
  Task Two      30 seconds
  Task Three    40 seconds
  Task Four     50 seconds

The computer has TWO identical processors that can
run in parallel. Each runs one task at a time, and
each task must run on a single processor.`;

const Q8 = `A job takes 140 seconds when run sequentially.

Run in parallel, the same job takes 70 seconds.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 21</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Nine questions &middot; 15 minutes &middot; Computer Systems and Networks
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The whole of Big Idea 4, which is three topics and about 11 to 15% of
            the multiple-choice section.</b> Activities 3.6 to 3.10.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Two of these are arithmetic</b> and they are the questions worth the
            most practice, because they are the only ones in this block where you can
            be confidently, checkably wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Set a timer for 15 minutes.</b> Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or{" "}
            <b>unsure</b>. Guess rather than leave a blank.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>Which of the following is <b>not</b> a computing device?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) A router</li>
            <li>(B) A smart sensor that reports temperature</li>
            <li>(C) An ethernet cable</li>
            <li>(D) A printer that can run its own diagnostics</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>
            The bandwidth of a computer network is best described as the maximum
            amount of data that can be sent in a fixed amount of time. It is usually
            measured in:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) bits per second</li>
            <li>(B) packets per path</li>
            <li>(C) devices per network</li>
            <li>(D) seconds per bit</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3</h3>

          <p>
            The internet uses <b>open</b> protocols. Which statement best describes
            what that means, and why it matters?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The protocols can be changed by any user at any time, so the network adapts quickly</li>
            <li>(B) The protocols are free to use but their rules are kept secret, so the network stays secure</li>
            <li>(C) Any device may join the network without being assigned an address</li>
            <li>(D) The rules are published and non-proprietary, so anyone can build a device that connects</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>Which of these are true of packets traveling across the internet?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) They may arrive at the destination out of order</li>
            <li>(B) They may not arrive at all</li>
            <li>(C) They contain metadata used for routing and for reassembly</li>
            <li>(D) All packets in one message follow the same path</li>
            <li>(E) A packet's data cannot be split across more than one packet</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            Which statement about the internet and the World Wide Web is accurate?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The internet is a system of linked pages, and the Web is the hardware that carries them</li>
            <li>(B) The World Wide Web is a system of linked pages, programs and files, and it uses the internet</li>
            <li>(C) They are two names for the same network</li>
            <li>(D) The internet uses the World Wide Web to route data between networks</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6</h3>

          <p>
            A network engineer wants to make a network more fault tolerant. Which
            change most directly achieves that?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Increasing the bandwidth of the busiest link</li>
            <li>(B) Replacing UDP with TCP throughout the network</li>
            <li>(C) Reducing the number of devices so there are fewer things to fail</li>
            <li>(D) Adding a second path between two devices that currently have only one</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 7</h3>

          <Code code={Q7} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Four Tasks, Two Processors" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <p>
            Which best approximates the <b>minimum possible time</b> to run all four
            tasks?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) 50 seconds</li>
            <li>(B) 70 seconds</li>
            <li>(C) 90 seconds</li>
            <li>(D) 140 seconds</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 8</h3>

          <Code code={Q8} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Speedup" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <p>What is the speedup of the parallel solution?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) 0.50</li>
            <li>(B) 1.75</li>
            <li>(C) 2.00</li>
            <li>(D) 70.00</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 9</h3>

          <p>
            A research problem requires more memory than any single available computer
            has. Which computational model addresses this, and why?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Distributed computing, because it uses multiple devices and so is not limited to one machine's resources</li>
            <li>(B) Parallel computing, because multiple processors can each hold part of the data</li>
            <li>(C) Sequential computing, because processing one item at a time uses the least memory</li>
            <li>(D) Parallel computing, because its speedup grows without limit as processors are added</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Nine answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far you
            got when the timer went off. It is collected as <b>Homework Drop 9</b>, together with Debug Challenge: Parallel Timing. <b>The drop is worth 5 points</b> for everything in it, graded on completion.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And show your working for Questions 7 and 8.</b> For Question 7, write
            down which tasks you put on which processor.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
